00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:06.289 align:start position:0%
in 1879 Thomas Edison perfected his most  iconic invention the incandescent light  bulb it was nice while it lasted in  September the European Union outlawed  100 watt light bulbs imposing fines of  up to 70 thousand dollars on law  breakers over the next few years bans on  lower wattage incandescence will kick in  eventually resigning Edison signature  invention to the dustbin of history  similar legislation kicks in in the  United States in 2012 the goal is to  jumpstart the market for compact  fluorescent light bulbs or cfls CFLs use  less electricity than conventional light  bulbs and are pitched as a way to  decrease energy use they also come with  a load of problems for starters CFLs can  take up to three minutes to warm up and  work best if left on for more than 15  minutes they cost up to 10 times as much  as incandescent and while they're  supposed to last up to 15 times longer  than an incandescent frequent turning on  and off decreases the lifespan today's  CFLs contain mercury which makes  disposal a problem and some interfere  with radios and other electronic  equipment most of them can't be used in  hanging light fixtures and closed  fixtures dimmer switches there with  garage door openers or ceiling fans or  other things that vibrate and the light  they cast is inferior but like it or not  Edison's most ubiquitous invention will  start to become illegal in the US come  2012 the latest bright idea from  regulators that increasingly tell us  what we can and can't do in public and  in private from smoking to trans fat to  gambling it's all been banned somewhere  for over a hundred years the light bulb  was synonymous with genius now it's just  one more symbol of the nanny state for  reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie
 

